From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Tue, 16 Aug 94 12:16:32 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-187] EPIST: certainty again 

Ed Regis wrote:
>Anyway, applying all this to epistemology, my view is that we can
>have objective knowledge regarding empirical matters, irrespective of
>whether or not the relevant measurements are expressible in whole
>numbers.  (Or at least you haven't shown anything to the contrary.)
>And we can also have objective knowledge in theoretical mathematics,
>whether we're dealing with the integers, the irrationals, or whatever
>else.  (Ditto.)

I agree with you on this, and in general, I mostly agree with
everything you said prior to this. I still shy away from the
application of the word "absolute" to the word "certainty", but
fortunately for me, I don't think objective knowledge requires
absolute certainty. I understand that you don't think my metaphor is
helpful, but since it's only a metaphor, there's not much in the way
of analytical argument I can do to defend it. The original post
(including the part about the "three significant digits") will just
have to stand on its own merits, or lack of them.

>The analytical distinction that I sought to preserve (and still do)
>is between a given quantity, x, and another quantity, y, that
>approaches x asymptotically.  I mistakenely thought that in your
>previous post you were trying to collapse that distinction.  In fact,
>you were not.  But now you *do* seem to be trying to collapse it!!!

No, I simply misunderstood you, and then complicated matters by
wording that last paragraph poorly to boot. 0.9999999999999... (what
would be read "zero point nine bar" if I had the right typography,
that is, there's an infinite number of 9's after the decimal point) is
the same number as 1, as any calculus teacher will tell you, but I
think you mean something different from this by your quantities x and
y. I'm not prepared to go into this particular subpoint any deeper,
since it has little to do with perception and critical techniques, and
a lot more to do with analysis (I like algebra better anyway).

In short, I don't think we're disagreeing on much anymore, except
perhaps the original points about whether absolute certainty is
possible, and whether absolute certainty is necessary for there to be
objective knowledge. I say no on both, but if I didn't say no on the
second one, I'd certainly *want* to say yes on the first.

As far as I'm concerned, it's a plain fact that objective knowledge is
possible. The task of philosophy is to explain *how* it is possible.
Philosophers who try to claim that objective knowledge is impossible
are just acquiescing to their own explanatory failure.

One of the reasons I like Hume so much better than Wittgenstein and
Rorty is that I get the feeling from Hume that he understood his own
explanatory failure, and felt his achievement was to point out the
explanatory failure of his smug predecessors. But most of Hume's
recent followers have mistaken his failure for success, and try to
deny the fact of objective knowledge just because they can't
understand how it is possible, how it works.

