Subject: [#94-8-189] Innateness of language [was If Knowledge Limits Learning]

I think that what's been shown is that chimps don't grasp human rules of
syntax and possibly don't grasp some of the underlying fundamental ideas. I
saw a documentary this winter on one of those chimp (or bonobo?) projects,
with an experiment that might support the connection between syntax and
social relationships.

The researchers had set up a simple experiment: one chimp faced another
across a window. The chimps were used to working at this window and getting
candy in dishes as a reward for participation in this setup. For example,
the chimp might be shown a number, point to the dish with the correct
number of candies, and be given the dish if he was right. For this test,
the "rule" was simple: the test chimp was shown two dishes of candy. She
would point at one dish, on some sort of cue, and that dish would be given
to the OTHER chimp. Over and over, the chimp would point to the dish with
more candy, end up with less candy, and show signs of displeasure.

(I have this vague recollection that gorillas *could* learn this sort
of thing...?)


